# Markdown documentation style (Scholar@UC)

Audience: new junior developers (about a 12th-grade reading level). They may be new to this stack — **not** foolish. Be clear and respectful. Do not talk down.

Primary local setup entry point: `docs/local/README.md` (multi-page, step-by-step). Root `README.md` stays mostly upstream Hyku; UC detail lives under `docs/local/`.

## Voice and clarity

- Short sentences. Plain words. One idea per step when possible.
- Prefer concrete actions (“Run this command”) over abstract theory.
- State **Expected:** outcomes after checks (what success looks like).
- Use branching when needed: “If X → go to Step N. If Y → …”
- Call out differences from Scholar@UC `develop` when it matters (Postgres vs MySQL, ports, etc.).
- Do **not** add patronizing banners like “do not paste this whole page” or “run one command at a time” as a lecture. Structure the page so steps are obvious instead.

## Commands in the terminal

- When the reader must decide based on output, give **one command** (or one clear choice), then the next instruction.
- Multi-line / chained commands are OK only when they are a fixed, always-together sequence with no judgment (e.g. start Sidekiq with fixed flags).
- Show the exact command in a fenced code block with **no language tag** (opening line is exactly ` ``` ` with nothing after it). Do **not** use `bash`, `sh`, or `text` — language tags turn on syntax highlighting, and `$VAR` / `$(…)` often become nearly invisible in Cursor/VS Code preview. A bare fence keeps copy-paste commands readable.
- Prefer copy-pasteable commands; avoid fake paths like `/path/to/ucrate` inside paste blocks when the reader should already be in the clone.

## Preview readability (critical)

Markdown preview in Cursor/VS Code often makes **links that wrap inline code** unreadable (dark link color on dark code background).

**Never** write: `[`.filename`](url)` or `[`path/to/file`](url)`.

**Do** write:

```markdown
<!-- filename as plain code; link with normal prose -->
copy `.env.local.mac.example` to `.env.local.mac`. [Open the template file](../../.env.local.mac.example).

<!-- or link with plain text, no backticks inside [] -->
See [.env.local.mac.example](../../.env.local.mac.example).
```

Same rule for `.solr_wrapper`, workflow paths, etc.

## Page structure

- Link **Back to** the setup index and **Next** to the following step.
- Keep “first-time setup” vs “everyday start” on separate pages when that helps (`start-services.md`, etc.).
- Put failure remedies in `troubleshooting.md` (or a short link there), not long digressions mid-checklist.

## Hyku vs UC content

- Prefer stock Hyku behavior. Local workarounds belong in docs and small, clearly removable config — not scattered app changes.
- Env for no-Docker local: document `.env.local.mac.example` → personal `.env.local.mac` (never tell people to source Docker `.env` for local Mac runs).
